{:page {:kind :post, :folder "adding-cleanup-color-and-movement-to-blog-themes"}, :text {:title "BareBlog v0.2.0: Adding cleanup, color and movement to blog themes", :blocks [{:kind :text, :content ["BareBlog v0.2.0 has just been released. It contains a few handy tools and visual possibilities to set." " " "Let's start with the visual possibilities. You can set any of these options in your blog.edn file."]} {:kind :list, :items [{:blocks [{:kind :plain, :content [{:kind :code, :content [":navbar-colors"]} " lets the colours of the theme drift behind the navbar"]}]} {:blocks [{:kind :plain, :content [{:kind :code, :content [":page-colors"]} " sets similar colors still behind the whole page"]}]} {:blocks [{:kind :plain, :content [{:kind :code, :content [":gradient-titles"]} " fills the titles of posts and pages, with a gradient of the colours of the theme. The title of a post moves its gradient once as the page opens."]}]}]} {:kind :text, :content ["The idea of these options is that it ads both color and movement to your blog, making it more alive. To further support this each blog post now has a line at the top of the page that grows wider as you scroll down the blog post."]} {:kind :text, :content ["Next to each blog post is now a content view that shows all headings, and marks where you are reading now. You can also use it to quickly navigate to a different part of the blog post."]} {:kind :text, :content ["Finally, on the presentation side post title font sizes are slightly increased."]} {:kind :text, :content ["All of these changes are meant to (optionally) add color and animation to a blog. You can see these features implemented in my own blog. I created them in the first place, because I like them."]} {:kind :text, :content ["I've also noticed that my use of tags was a bit random, for example I used " {:kind :code, :content ["ClojureScript"]} " and " {:kind :code, :content ["Clojurescript"]} ", which end up becoming two different tags even though they point to the same thing. To aide in that I've added a few command line tools to help clean up tags. Run " {:kind :code, :content ["bareblog help"]} " to see the possibilities. The commands specifically for tags are:"]} {:kind :code, :text "bareblog tags                    ;; every tag, and each written in more than one way\nbareblog tags rename old \"new\"   ;; a tag, in every post and page\n              --yes              ;;  with a yes to the question\n"} {:kind :text, :content ["I hope these options are helpful. Enjoy!"]}], :tags ["BareBlog" "release" "color" "animation"], :summary "BareBlog v0.2.0 has just been released. It contains a few handy tools and visual possibilities to set. Let's start with the visual possibilities. You can set any of these options in your blog.edn file.", :date "2026-10-23T18:04:52+02:00", :folder "adding-cleanup-color-and-movement-to-blog-themes"}}